 Configure mingw + Visual Studio Code for Windows API Development
Learning Objectives
By completing this lab, you will:

Install and configure mingw-w64 cross-compiler on Linux
Set up Visual Studio Code for Windows API development
Create and compile a Windows API "Hello World" program
Verify cross-compilation functionality for Windows targets
Prerequisites
Basic knowledge of C programming
Familiarity with command-line operations
Understanding of compilation concepts
Lab Environment
Al Nafi provides Linux-based cloud machines for this lab. Simply click Start Lab to access your dedicated Linux machine. The provided machine is bare metal with no pre-installed development tools, so you will install all required components during this lab.

Task 1: Install mingw-w64 and Configure Environment
Subtask 1.1: Update System and Install mingw-w64
# Update package manager
sudo apt update

# Install mingw-w64 cross-compiler
sudo apt install -y mingw-w64 mingw-w64-tools

# Verify installation
x86_64-w64-mingw32-gcc --version
Subtask 1.2: Configure Environment Variables
# Add mingw to PATH (temporary for current session)
export PATH="/usr/bin:$PATH"

# Make permanent by adding to .bashrc
echo 'export MINGW_PREFIX=x86_64-w64-mingw32' >> ~/.bashrc
echo 'export CC=$MINGW_PREFIX-gcc' >> ~/.bashrc
echo 'export CXX=$MINGW_PREFIX-g++' >> ~/.bashrc

# Reload environment
source ~/.bashrc
Subtask 1.3: Verify mingw Installation
# Check available mingw tools
ls /usr/bin/*mingw32*

# Test basic compilation capability
echo 'int main(){return 0;}' > test.c
x86_64-w64-mingw32-gcc test.c -o test.exe
file test.exe
rm test.c test.exe
Task 2: Set up Visual Studio Code for Windows Development
Subtask 2.1: Install Visual Studio Code
# Download and install VS Code
wget -qO- https://packages.microsoft.com/keys/microsoft.asc | gpg --dearmor > packages.microsoft.gpg
sudo install -o root -g root -m 644 packages.microsoft.gpg /etc/apt/trusted.gpg.d/
sudo sh -c 'echo "deb [arch=amd64,arm64,armhf signed-by=/etc/apt/trusted.gpg.d/packages.microsoft.gpg] https://packages.microsoft.com/repos/code stable main" > /etc/apt/sources.list.d/vscode.list'

sudo apt update
sudo apt install -y code
Subtask 2.2: Create Project Directory Structure
# Create project directory
mkdir -p ~/windows-api-project
cd ~/windows-api-project

# Create source directory
mkdir src
mkdir build
Subtask 2.3: Configure VS Code Settings
# Create .vscode directory for project settings
mkdir .vscode

# Create tasks.json for build configuration
cat > .vscode/tasks.json << 'EOF'
{
    "version": "2.0.0",
    "tasks": [
        {
            "label": "build-windows",
            "type": "shell",
            "command": "x86_64-w64-mingw32-gcc",
            "args": [
                "-o",
                "build/app.exe",
                "src/*.c",
                "-luser32",
                "-lgdi32",
                "-lkernel32"
            ],
            "group": {
                "kind": "build",
                "isDefault": true
            },
            "presentation": {
                "echo": true,
                "reveal": "always",
                "focus": false,
                "panel": "shared"
            }
        }
    ]
}
EOF

# Create c_cpp_properties.json for IntelliSense
cat > .vscode/c_cpp_properties.json << 'EOF'
{
    "configurations": [
        {
            "name": "MinGW-w64",
            "includePath": [
                "${workspaceFolder}/**",
                "/usr/x86_64-w64-mingw32/include/**"
            ],
            "defines": [
                "_WIN32",
                "UNICODE",
                "_UNICODE"
            ],
            "compilerPath": "/usr/bin/x86_64-w64-mingw32-gcc",
            "cStandard": "c11",
            "intelliSenseMode": "gcc-x64"
        }
    ],
    "version": 4
}
EOF
Task 3: Test Setup with Windows API "Hello World" Program
Subtask 3.1: Create Windows API Hello World Program
# Create the main source file
cat > src/main.c << 'EOF'
#include <windows.h>

int WINAPI WinMain(HINSTANCE hInstance, HINSTANCE hPrevInstance, 
                   LPSTR lpCmdLine, int nCmdShow) {
    MessageBoxA(NULL, "Hello World from Windows API!", "Success", MB_OK);
    return 0;
}
EOF
Subtask 3.2: Create Console Version for Testing
# Create console version for easier testing
cat > src/console.c << 'EOF'
#include <stdio.h>
#include <windows.h>

int main() {
    printf("Hello World from Windows API Console!\n");
    printf("This program was cross-compiled on Linux for Windows.\n");
    
    // Test Windows API function
    DWORD version = GetVersion();
    printf("Windows version info available.\n");
    
    return 0;
}
EOF
Subtask 3.3: Compile and Test Programs
# Compile GUI version
x86_64-w64-mingw32-gcc -o build/hello_gui.exe src/main.c -luser32 -lgdi32 -lkernel32

# Compile console version
x86_64-w64-mingw32-gcc -o build/hello_console.exe src/console.c -lkernel32

# Verify executables were created
ls -la build/
file build/*.exe
Subtask 3.4: Test VS Code Integration
# Open project in VS Code
code .
In VS Code:

Open src/main.c
Press Ctrl+Shift+P and type "Tasks: Run Build Task"
Select "build-windows"
Verify compilation succeeds in terminal
Subtask 3.5: Create Build Script
# Create automated build script
cat > build.sh << 'EOF'
#!/bin/bash

echo "Building Windows API project..."

# Create build directory if it doesn't exist
mkdir -p build

# Compile GUI application
echo "Compiling GUI application..."
x86_64-w64-mingw32-gcc -o build/hello_gui.exe src/main.c -luser32 -lgdi32 -lkernel32

# Compile console application
echo "Compiling console application..."
x86_64-w64-mingw32-gcc -o build/hello_console.exe src/console.c -lkernel32

# Check if builds were successful
if [ -f "build/hello_gui.exe" ] && [ -f "build/hello_console.exe" ]; then
    echo "Build successful!"
    echo "Files created:"
    ls -la build/*.exe
else
    echo "Build failed!"
    exit 1
fi
EOF

# Make script executable
chmod +x build.sh

# Test build script
./build.sh
Verification Steps
Verify Complete Setup
# Check mingw installation
which x86_64-w64-mingw32-gcc

# Verify VS Code installation
code --version

# Check project structure
tree ~/windows-api-project

# Verify executables
file build/*.exe | grep "PE32+"
Test Cross-Compilation Capabilities
# Create advanced Windows API test
cat > src/advanced_test.c << 'EOF'
#include <windows.h>
#include <stdio.h>

int main() {
    // Test various Windows API functions
    SYSTEM_INFO sysInfo;
    GetSystemInfo(&sysInfo);
    
    printf("Cross-compilation test successful!\n");
    printf("Target architecture: %s\n", 
           (sysInfo.wProcessorArchitecture == PROCESSOR_ARCHITECTURE_AMD64) ? 
           "x64" : "x86");
    
    return 0;
}
EOF

# Compile advanced test
x86_64-w64-mingw32-gcc -o build/advanced_test.exe src/advanced_test.c -lkernel32

# Verify compilation
echo "Advanced test compilation: $([ -f build/advanced_test.exe ] && echo 'SUCCESS' || echo 'FAILED')"
Troubleshooting Common Issues
Issue: mingw-w64 not found

# Reinstall mingw-w64
sudo apt remove mingw-w64
sudo apt update
sudo apt install mingw-w64
Issue: VS Code IntelliSense not working

# Install C/C++ extension
code --install-extension ms-vscode.cpptools
Issue: Build fails with linking errors

# Check library linking order
x86_64-w64-mingw32-gcc src/main.c -luser32 -lgdi32 -lkernel32 -o build/test.exe
Conclusion
You have successfully configured a complete Windows API development environment on Linux using mingw-w64 and Visual Studio Code. This setup enables you to:

Cross-compile Windows applications from Linux
Develop Windows API programs with full IntelliSense support
Build both console and GUI Windows applications
Use modern development tools for Windows-targeted software
This cross-platform development approach is valuable for creating Windows software in Linux environments, supporting CI/CD pipelines, and maintaining consistent development workflows across different operating systems.
